  "label": "About the Author",
  "title": "ABOUT THE AUTHOR",
  "shortTitle": "About the Author",
  "file": "chapters/36-about-author.json",
  "index": 36,
  "sourceRange": [
    1753,
    1769
  ],
  "elements": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "LALIT R MISHRA writes from the space between thought and observation. "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1754
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "His work explores the psychological patterns that shape human emotion — "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1755
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "especially the silent loops of memory, attachment, and meaning-making that "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1756
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "people often live inside without realizing. "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1757
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Rather than focusing on events or stories, his writing focuses on the internal "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1758
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "structure of experience itself — how the mind repeats, distorts, and "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1759
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "reconstructs reality until it begins to feel personal. "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1760
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "He does not offer answers or instruction. "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1761
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "Instead, his perspective is built on a simple belief:  "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1762
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "“Until you understand your own mind, the world will always feel like something "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1763
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "happening to you, not something you are part of understanding.” "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1764
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "His writing encourages awareness over reaction, observation over assumption, "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1765
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "and self-recognition over external dependence. "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1766
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "This book continues that approach — not as guidance, but as a mirror to what "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1767
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "already exists within thought. "
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1768
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "runs": [
        {
          "text": "— LALIT R MISHRA"
        }
      ],
      "sourceParagraph": 1769,
      "role": "chapter-end"
    }
  ],
